        "id":  "patna-nursing-students-lead-organ-donation-awareness",
        "title":  "Patna Nursing Students Lead Organ Donation Awareness",
        "summary":  "“One Pledge, Many Lives: Patna’s Nursing Students Show the Way for Organ Donation” Most people think of nurses as silent workers in white coats. But on a recent afternoon at IGIMS Patna,",
        "content":  "\u003cp\u003e“One Pledge, Many Lives: Patna’s Nursing Students Show the Way for Organ Donation”\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMost people think of nurses as silent workers in white coats. But on a recent afternoon at IGIMS Patna, these young students were anything but silent.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThey were creating art. They were absorbing life-saving knowledge. More importantly, they were stepping up for something that can save lives long after ours end—organ donation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhy This Campaign Mattered\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis wasn’t just a classroom session. It was part of the national ‘Jeevan Sanjivani’ campaign, designed to spark awareness about organ donation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAt the College of Nursing, IGIMS Patna, students joined hands with doctors and faculty over two powerful days. Together, they made the topic personal, emotional, and urgent.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA Competition That Touched Hearts\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn day one, the college organised a poster-making competition. It was open to both BSc Nursing and GNM students. Rather than treating it as another college event, students gave it their all.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSome painted stories of loss and rebirth. Others created bold messages:“Don’t carry your organs to heaven—heaven knows we need them here.”\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe creativity was compelling. However, the emotions behind each poster were what made them unforgettable.Winners will be honoured on Organ Donation Day, but in truth, every participant contributed to a larger cause.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTurning Facts Into Purpose\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe second day moved the conversation forward. A special lecture was organised to help students understand how organ donation works and why it matters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDr. Anuja Daniel, Principal of the College of Nursing, opened the session. She reminded students that awareness drives like these are essential—not just for learning, but for leadership.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMoreover, she explained that nurses play a crucial role in organ donation—from identifying potential donors to counselling families. In her words, Bihar has the potential to lead, just like other progressive states, if it commits to spreading this message.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEncouraging the Next Generation of Changemakers\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFollowing that, Dr. Manish Mandal, Chairman of SOTO, spoke directly to the students.He talked about how organ donation is managed under the National Organ Transplant Program, and how science is progressing every single day.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eUnlike typical lectures, his words were direct. He urged students not just to listen but to research, write, and initiate conversations. He believed young minds can build new momentum for the movement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMaking the Pledge Real\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThen came IEC Consultant Ekta, who explained the process in simple terms.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnyone above 18, she said, can pledge their organs online. To do so, one simply needs to visit www.notto.abdm.gov.in. She clarified doubts, broke myths, and gave students a clear call to action.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSeveral students decided to pledge that very moment. Others promised to discuss it at home. Clearly, something shifted in the room that day.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eClosing a Chapter, Opening a Mission\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe event closed with a warm vote of thanks from Dr. Rupashree Dasgupta, Vice Principal of the College. Faculty members, SOTO’s transplant coordinator Jaspal Singh, and program assistant Seema Kumari were also present.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhile the formal program ended, the sense of responsibility stayed behind. The students left with more than certificates or applause—they left with conviction.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNow, they are ready to be ambassadors of change, not just in hospitals, but in society.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDon’t Wait to Make a Difference\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIf you’re over 18, take a moment. Visit www.notto.abdm.gov.in. One pledge can save up to eight lives.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnd if stories like this move you, don’t let them go unnoticed.Follow www.biharsay.com for regular stories from Bihar’s ground—where change is not just hoped for, but made real by ordinary people like these students.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBecome a part of our 12,000+ global community. Because Bihar is changing—and you deserve to see how.\u003c/p\u003e",
