        "id":  "bpsc-re-examination-today-amid-tight-security-ongoing-protests",
        "title":  "BPSC Re-Examination Today Amid Protests: All Eyes on Patna as Security Tightens",
        "summary":  "When exams determine futures, fairness is non-negotiable. Today, the Bihar Public Service Commission (BPSC) is conducting a re-examination for 12,000 candidates in Patna, following serious allegations of irregularities during the December 13",
        "content":  "\u003cp\u003eWhen exams determine futures, fairness is non-negotiable. Today, the Bihar Public Service Commission (BPSC) is conducting a re-examination for 12,000 candidates in Patna, following serious allegations of irregularities during the December 13 exam. This crucial step aims to restore trust amidst mounting protests and heightened security.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhy the Re-Examination?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe re-exam specifically addresses concerns about the December test conducted at Bapu Pariksha Parisar, where procedural lapses were reported. Authorities have now split the candidates across 22 centres in Patna to ensure better management and transparency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eUnprecedented Security Measures\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn a bid to prevent disruptions, Patna District Magistrate Gaurav Kumar has invoked Section 163 of the Criminal Procedure Code. This bans public gatherings of more than five people within a 100-metre radius of the exam centres. Demonstrations, processions, and even the use of loudspeakers are prohibited.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe restrictions will remain in effect from 8 AM to 5 PM today, particularly impacting prominent centres like P.N. Anglo Sanskrit High School and Government Girls High Secondary School in Shastri Nagar. Candidates are barred from carrying mobile phones or smart devices, ensuring that the examination environment remains secure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Protests Continue\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDespite these efforts, discontent brews. On Friday, thousands of students took to the streets, disrupting rail and road traffic. Left-aligned student organisations even attempted to march to the Chief Minister’s residence. The protests escalated further as police resorted to force, leaving several demonstrators injured.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAdding fuel to the fire, Jan Suraj Party leader Prashant Kishor has begun an indefinite hunger strike at Gandhi Maidan. He demands accountability from the police and calls for the cancellation of the exam altogether.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA Critical Test for Authorities and Aspirants\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAs candidates prepare to rewrite their future, the authorities face the challenge of conducting a smooth and fair examination. The stakes are high for everyone involved.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eStay updated on this developing story. Follow www.biharsay.com for real-time updates and join our growing community of over 10,000 engaged readers worldwide!\u003c/p\u003e",
