        "summary":  "Biharâ€™s Gatka Revolution: A Teacher, His Students, and Five Shining Medals â€œI feel like picking up a wooden stick and playing Gatka myself!â€ said young Jainab Parveen after watching Biharâ€™s athletes in action. This simple ...",
        "content":  "\u003cp\u003eBiharâ€™s Gatka Revolution: A Teacher, His Students, and Five Shining Medals\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eâ€œI feel like picking up a wooden stick and playing Gatka myself!â€ said young Jainab Parveen after watching Biharâ€™s athletes in action. This simple yet powerful statement captures the spirit of the Khelo India Youth Games (KIYG) 2025 hosted in Bihar â€” where dreams met tradition and left the nation inspired.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn a defining moment for Biharâ€™s sporting landscape, the state proudly claimed five medals in Gatka â€” a traditional martial art rooted in Sikh history. With a silver and four bronze medals, Bihar proved it is no longer just a participant but a rising force in Indiaâ€™s indigenous sports scene.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA Teacherâ€™s Vision Sparks a Movement\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRavi Roshan, a dedicated middle-school teacher from Gaya, didnâ€™t just teach â€” he ignited ambition.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHe brought his students to the IIM Campus to witness the thrill of Gatka firsthand. What started as a field trip turned into a medal haul. â€œI just hoped Bihar would win one medal. I never imagined five!â€ he said, beaming with pride.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHis students, many watching a live sporting event for the first time, were instantly captivated. This exposure didnâ€™t just fuel passion; it triggered purpose. For many, this was the moment they realized Biharâ€™s growing potential in lesser-known sports like Gatka and Mallakhamb.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMeet the Medal Winners Who Made History\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe athletes didnâ€™t just compete; they conquered. Hereâ€™s how Bihar left its mark:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnshu won a silver medal in the Girlsâ€™ Single Soti event, finishing just behind Punjabâ€™s Tamanna. Akash Kumar Sharma earned bronze in Boysâ€™ Farri Soti. Komal Jain took bronze in Girlsâ€™ Farri Soti. Biharâ€™s boys and girls teams secured bronze in Farri Soti Team Events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThese victories were not only a boost to the athletes but a testament to the efforts being made to popularize traditional martial arts beyond their regional strongholds.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMaharashtraâ€™s First-Ever Medal \u0026 A Growing Movement\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt wasnâ€™t just Bihar that made headlines. Maharashtra too celebrated its first-ever Gatka medal in the girlsâ€™ team event. Coach Arti Chaudhary credited their success to the athletesâ€™ adaptability and background in Mardani, Maharashtraâ€™s indigenous martial art.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe energy at the IIM Campus was electric. Students chanted from the stands, turning the venue into a celebration of heritage, grit, and new beginnings.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGatka: Reviving Roots, Shaping Futures\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGatka isnâ€™t just a sport â€” itâ€™s a cultural symbol. Backed by the Central Government and supported by Prime Minister Narendra Modi himself, it stands at the confluence of history and athleticism.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDating back hundreds of years and deeply embedded in Sikh tradition, Gatka is now finding new life across states like Bihar, Maharashtra, and Jharkhand â€” thanks to platforms like KIYG and passionate educators like Ravi Roshan.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhy This Matters More Than Medals\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis story isnâ€™t just about five medals. Itâ€™s about inspiration. Itâ€™s about an ecosystem that supports ambition, embraces tradition, and drives transformation â€” one student, one teacher, one stick at a time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSo, if you think Bihar is only about mainstream cricket or academics, think again. Itâ€™s where indigenous pride meets global recognition â€” and itâ€™s only the beginning.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eðŸ‘‰ Want to know more stories that change the narrative of Bihar? Follow www.biharsay.com â€” Biharâ€™s leading apolitical storytelling platform. Be part of our 11k+ strong global community as we spotlight the real Bihar â€” from sports to grassroots heroes, untold traditions to bold transformations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBihar Say â€” Bihar Ki Kahani, Duniya Ke Liye!\u003c/p\u003e",
