        "title":  "This GI-Tagged Rice from Bihar is Now Winning Hearts Abroad",
        "summary":  "Why This Rice from Bihar is Now Winning Hearts Abroad If you think rice is just rice, you havenâ€™t heard of Katarni from Bhagalpur, Bihar.This isnâ€™t your ordinary grainâ€”this is aroma, tradition, and pride packed into every bi...",
        "content":  "\u003cp\u003eWhy This Rice from Bihar is Now Winning Hearts Abroad\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIf you think rice is just rice, you havenâ€™t heard of Katarni from Bhagalpur, Bihar.This isnâ€™t your ordinary grainâ€”this is aroma, tradition, and pride packed into every bite. And now, itâ€™s no longer confined to Biharâ€™s kitchens or festive occasions. It has crossed borders, reaching international markets and redefining Indiaâ€™s rice legacy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLetâ€™s uncover whatâ€™s so royal about this humble yet heritage-rich rice.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eKatarni: From Local Grains to Global Fame\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA few years ago, Katarni rice was grown in limited patches of around 500 acres. Its reach was local, mostly sold during festivals or special occasions. Back then, farmers struggled for fair prices and visibility.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eToday, everythingâ€™s changed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThanks to the Geographical Indication (GI) Tag, Katarni rice has gained not just recognition but real value. Cultivation has now spread over 3,000 acres, and the price has jumped from â‚¹60 to â‚¹150 per kilo. With that, farmersâ€™ income has doubled, sometimes even tripled.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis isnâ€™t just an economic winâ€”itâ€™s an identity revival.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhy Farmers Are Smiling in Bhagalpur\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe agriculture department and local governance worked together to boost this indigenous variety. They introduced modern farming techniques, quality seeds, and supported farmers with better market access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTransitioning from low-demand to a premium gourmet item, Katarni has helped revive traditional agriculture and local pride in Bhagalpur.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnd hereâ€™s the best partâ€”it isnâ€™t just about growing rice anymore. Itâ€™s about growing a brand from Bihar.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Secret? Aroma, Sweetness \u0026 Bhagalpurâ€™s Soil\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSo, what makes Katarni rice so desirable?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eItâ€™s incredibly aromaticâ€”you can smell it even before the first bite. Its sweet undertone makes it perfect for kheer, a classic Indian dessert. The texture? Soft, almost silky. Even the flattened rice (Katarni Chura) is so soft, you can eat it without soaking!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eItâ€™s incredibly aromaticâ€”you can smell it even before the first bite.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIts sweet undertone makes it perfect for kheer, a classic Indian dessert.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe texture? Soft, almost silky.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEven the flattened rice (Katarni Chura) is so soft, you can eat it without soaking!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis magic lies in the â€œkaali domat mittiâ€ (black alluvial soil) of Bhagalpur. Experts agreeâ€”only Bhagalpur-grown Katarni has this distinct aroma and taste.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eðŸ‡®ðŸ‡³ âœˆï¸ Why the World Is Now Buying Katarni\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith a global push towards sustainable, authentic food choices, the world is paying attention to regional specialties like Katarni rice.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eInternational buyers are now importing it for premium Indian cuisine, especially for restaurants that want authentic Bihari flavours on their menu.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eKatarni is now not just riceâ€”itâ€™s a story of soil, smell, and self-worth.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eKatarniâ€™s Rise = Biharâ€™s Rise\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhether itâ€™s Shahi Litchi, Jardalu Mango, Magahi Paan, Mithila Makhana, or now Katarni rice, Bihar is reclaiming its place on Indiaâ€™s agricultural map.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith every grain exported, a message is sent out:ðŸ‘‰ Bihar is not backward. Bihar is richâ€”in heritage, in produce, and in pride.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSo, the next time you eat kheer made with Katarni, know that youâ€™re tasting more than dessertâ€”youâ€™re tasting legacy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFollow www.biharsay.com for more such rooted yet global stories, andbecome part of our 11K+ strong global community.Bihar Say is not just a platformâ€”itâ€™s the digital voice of Bihar.ðŸŽ™ï¸ Donâ€™t miss BiharCastâ€”our podcast and video series uncovering voices that matter.\u003c/p\u003e",
