        "title":  "From 3 to 500+ Artists: iMithilaâ€™s Rise from Rural Bihar",
        "summary":  "How iMithila Turned a Motherâ€™s Dream into a Global Movement for Biharâ€™s Madhubani ArtistsPart of the â€œ60 Days â€“ 60 Startups from Biharâ€ series by Bihar Say â€œMaa ki yaadon se chali thi choti si kahaaniâ€¦ aaj poori duni...",
        "content":  "\u003cp\u003eHow iMithila Turned a Motherâ€™s Dream into a Global Movement for Biharâ€™s Madhubani ArtistsPart of the â€œ60 Days â€“ 60 Startups from Biharâ€ series by Bihar Say\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eâ€œMaa ki yaadon se chali thi choti si kahaaniâ€¦ aaj poori duniya us kahaani ko pehchaan rahi hai.â€When Biharâ€™s tradition met a daughterâ€™s mission, iMithila was bornâ€”not as a brand, but as a revival.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFrom a Motherâ€™s Legacy to a Movement: The Birth of iMithila (2013)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn 2013, what began as an emotional tribute to a motherâ€™s love for Madhubani art has now grown into a movement.Founded by IIT and IIM alumna, Nidhi Kumari Jha, iMithila emerged from a deep-rooted desire to honour her late motherâ€™s dreamâ€”to preserve, promote, and empower the Mithila painting tradition that had once been a staple of every rural home in North Bihar.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBack then, the idea of launching a startup from Biharâ€”especially one centred around folk artâ€”sounded risky. But Nidhi believed in something more powerful than numbers: her roots.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRather than chasing markets, she chose to build from Bihar for the world.What started with just 3 local women artists in Madhubani is now a thriving ecosystem of over 500+ rural artisansâ€”mostly womenâ€”who are earning dignified livelihoods while keeping a centuries-old tradition alive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFrom Villages to Vogue: Taking Mithila Art Global\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe real beauty of iMithila lies not just in its handpainted sarees, dupattas, and home dÃ©corâ€”but in the stories etched in every stroke.Each piece is not mass-produced; it is a canvas of culture, painted with hands that carry generations of knowledge and pain.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith a model rooted in empowerment and not charity, iMithila transformed artisans into entrepreneurs. They were trained, paid fair wages, and connected directly with buyers.This ensured that the earnings reached the hands that truly created the magic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn 2017, iMithila launched its official website and retail platform, bringing Biharâ€™s art to a global audience. The startup has since sold products across India, USA, UK, Australia, and Germany, proving that heritage can be a businessâ€”and a sustainable one at that.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe venture also partners with Amazon Karigar and various government initiatives to promote indigenous crafts. Yet, iMithila has retained its emotional core: keeping Biharâ€™s art relevant without diluting its soul.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen Empowerment Wears a Sari: Real Women, Real Impact\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMeet Sunita Devi from Ranti village. She never imagined her art could go beyond her courtyard. But now, thanks to iMithila, her handpainted dupattas are being worn in Delhi boardrooms and Paris boutiques.She no longer needs permission to dreamâ€”she has earnings, respect, and identity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOver the years, iMithila has empowered hundreds of such rural women, not only with income but with self-worth.Many are now educating their daughters, building homes, and even investing back in their own small art collectives.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe brandâ€™s growth isnâ€™t just economicâ€”itâ€™s deeply social.By reconnecting urban customers with rural stories, iMithila has become a cultural bridgeâ€”between tradition and innovation, between Bihar and the world.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Bigger Picture: Preserving Culture, Creating Futures\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn a time when AI-generated art floods timelines, iMithila reminds us of the value of the human hand and heart.Its business model is proof that art, when ethically and creatively scaled, can fight poverty, gender bias, and cultural erosionâ€”all at once.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNidhiâ€™s team continues to digitize ancient patterns, archive oral histories of Mithila art, and collaborate with design institutes to keep the craft evolving without losing authenticity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis is more than a startup storyâ€”itâ€™s Biharâ€™s answer to those who say tradition cannot be turned into transformation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eðŸ‘‰ Want more such unheard stories from Biharâ€™s soil?Join the movement. Follow www.biharsay.com to discover real entrepreneurs, grassroots change, and the soul of Indiaâ€™s next growth engine.\u003c/p\u003e",
