        "id":  "branded-the-taste-of-bihar-thekua-that-earned-in-lakhs",
        "title":  "Branded the Taste of Biharâ€”Thekua That Earned in Lakhs",
        "summary":  "â€œEk thekua ne badal diya ek parivaar ka jeevan.â€Sometimes, all it takes is one bite of nostalgia to create something extraordinary. A Motherâ€™s Recipe, A Sonâ€™s Vision: The Birth of Desi Tesi In April 2023, a small kitchen i...",
        "content":  "\u003cp\u003eâ€œEk thekua ne badal diya ek parivaar ka jeevan.â€Sometimes, all it takes is one bite of nostalgia to create something extraordinary.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA Motherâ€™s Recipe, A Sonâ€™s Vision: The Birth of Desi Tesi\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn April 2023, a small kitchen in Guwahati became the birthplace of a startup that would go on to make headlines across India. Founded by Tilak Pandit, a commerce student with no business degree or funding, Desi Tesi began with a single thought: What if the flavors of Bihar could be delivered to every home in India?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis wasnâ€™t just about foodâ€”it was about emotion, identity, and memory. Tilak knew that thekua, a traditional Bihari snack often made during Chhath Puja, held deep meaning for many. What he also knew was that outside Bihar, it was nearly impossible to find an authentic version.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSo, he did what few would dare: he teamed up with his mother, Chand Jyoti Devi, and started Desi Tesi with no factory, no capitalâ€”just a strong belief in the power of tradition.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMeet the Founders: Tilak Pandit and Chand Jyoti Devi\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAt the heart of Desi Tesi are two people: a mother and son duo with a shared dream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTilak Pandit, the CEO and co-founder, is the strategist, marketer, and face of the brand. Still in college when he launched the startup, he used social mediaâ€”especially Instagram marketingâ€”to attract attention, generate orders, and build a loyal customer base. Chand Jyoti Devi, the co-founder and manufacturing head, is the soul of Desi Tesi. With years of experience in making traditional Bihari snacks, she led the kitchen operations, taught other local women, and ensured that every productâ€”whether thekua, gujiya, or makhanaâ€”was handmade with love and authenticity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTilak Pandit, the CEO and co-founder, is the strategist, marketer, and face of the brand. Still in college when he launched the startup, he used social mediaâ€”especially Instagram marketingâ€”to attract attention, generate orders, and build a loyal customer base.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eChand Jyoti Devi, the co-founder and manufacturing head, is the soul of Desi Tesi. With years of experience in making traditional Bihari snacks, she led the kitchen operations, taught other local women, and ensured that every productâ€”whether thekua, gujiya, or makhanaâ€”was handmade with love and authenticity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTheir chemistry wasnâ€™t just familialâ€”it was entrepreneurial.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDesi Tesiâ€™s Growth: From Local Orders to â‚¹12 Lakh+ Turnover\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe growth wasnâ€™t immediate, but it was steadyâ€”and heartfelt. Initially, Desi Tesi began with small batches of thekua made at home. But soon, word spread. People from Assam, Delhi, Maharashtra, and even southern states began ordering.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat made it unique?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e100% handmade snacks Zero preservatives Rooted in traditional recipes Flavors that reminded people of their childhood\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e100% handmade snacks\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eZero preservatives\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRooted in traditional recipes\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFlavors that reminded people of their childhood\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThanks to Tilakâ€™s use of Instagram reels and storytelling, the brand attracted both NRIs and Indians across metro cities who were craving a taste of home. Within a year, Desi Tesi generated over â‚¹12 lakh in turnover, without ever stepping into a factory setup.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Mission Behind Desi Tesi: More Than Just Snacks\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhile thekua was the beginning, Desi Tesi was always meant to be more than just a food business. Tilak and his mother had a vision much bigger than profits.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHereâ€™s what theyâ€™re building:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePreserving Biharâ€™s Food HeritageLost snacks like lai, anarsa, and khaja are next in line. Desi Tesi aims to revive and modernize these recipes for todayâ€™s generation. Launching 100% Natural ProductsTheir new line includes flavored makhana, natural honey, and other healthy, preservative-free offeringsâ€”balancing wellness with taste. Empowering Local HandsWomen from local communities in Guwahati and parts of Bihar are being trained and employed. The goal is to build a self-sustaining ecosystem that rewards local talent. Taking Biharâ€™s Taste GlobalFrom Guwahati to Goa and eventually to global markets, Desi Tesi wants to make Bihar ka swaad a global food identity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePreserving Biharâ€™s Food HeritageLost snacks like lai, anarsa, and khaja are next in line. Desi Tesi aims to revive and modernize these recipes for todayâ€™s generation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLaunching 100% Natural ProductsTheir new line includes flavored makhana, natural honey, and other healthy, preservative-free offeringsâ€”balancing wellness with taste.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEmpowering Local HandsWomen from local communities in Guwahati and parts of Bihar are being trained and employed. The goal is to build a self-sustaining ecosystem that rewards local talent.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTaking Biharâ€™s Taste GlobalFrom Guwahati to Goa and eventually to global markets, Desi Tesi wants to make Bihar ka swaad a global food identity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLessons for Every Aspiring Entrepreneur\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDesi Tesiâ€™s journey shows that:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou donâ€™t need a degree to start a business. You donâ€™t need capital if you have culture. You donâ€™t need investors if you believe in your roots.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou donâ€™t need a degree to start a business.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou donâ€™t need capital if you have culture.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou donâ€™t need investors if you believe in your roots.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eInstead, all you need is a vision, a support system (even if itâ€™s your mom), and the courage to start small.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDesi Tesi has already changed the way India looks at regional food entrepreneurship. And for every small-town dreamer thinking â€œmain kya kar sakta hoon?â€ â€” this story is the answer.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eðŸ‘‰ Inspired by Desi Tesi?Join the â€œ60 Days â€“ 60 Startups from Biharâ€ series at www.biharsay.com and discover more such stories. Be part of our growing 12K+ global community that supports real changemakers from the heartland.\u003c/p\u003e",
